{
 "slug": "integration",
 "title": "Integration and Areas",
 "seo_title": "Year 12 A Level Maths: Integration Worksheets",
 "short_title": "integration",
 "domain": "Pure Mathematics",
 "standard_code": "A level Mathematics: Pure mathematics",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/gce-as-and-a-level-mathematics",
 "standard_text": "Know and use the fundamental theorem of calculus; integrate xⁿ (excluding n = −1) and related sums; evaluate definite integrals; use a definite integral to find the area under a curve.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 12 A level maths worksheets on integration: integrating powers of x, the constant of integration, definite integrals and areas under curves. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹ ÷ (n + 1) + c.",
 "key_sub": "Area = definite integral (top limit minus bottom limit).",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>Integration reverses differentiation: add 1 to the power, then divide by the new power. Indefinite integrals need + <i>c</i>.</p><p>∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(<i>x</i>) d<i>x</i> = F(<i>b</i>) − F(<i>a</i>) gives the area between the curve and the <i>x</i>-axis (above the axis).</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>∫<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> 3<i>x</i>² d<i>x</i></p><p class='sn-ex-s'>[<i>x</i>³] from 0 to 2</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>8 − 0</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 8</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Multiplying by the new power instead of dividing.</li><li>Forgetting + c.</li><li>Working out F(a) − F(b).</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "Why do you add c?",
   "a": "Because differentiating any constant gives 0, so many functions have the same derivative."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you find the area under a curve?",
   "a": "Integrate the function and evaluate it between the limits: F(b) − F(a)."
  }
 ],
